    "case_id": "283",
    "case_summary": "A man in his 60s presented with a lesion on the left lower eyelid. His ocular history was notable for a traumatic icicle injury as a child that resulted in loss of the left eye. He had surgery to place an implant into the eye socket, and approximately 14 years prior underwent a lower eyelid reconstruction. He had excellent prosthesis position without need for refitting or replacement for more than 10 years until recently noting that his ocular prosthesis would spontaneously extrude from his anophthalmic socket.\n\nOn examination, he had a small, pearly lesion in the tear trough region of the left lower eyelid that had been growing over the past few months. He had contraction of the conjunctival mucosal tissue across the fornix of the left lower eyelid and increased scleral show of his ocular prosthesis with shortening of his lower eyelid skin. His prosthesis was extruded from his socket due to cicatricial effects causing conjunctival shrinkage and eyelid skin contracture.\n\n- Imaging:  Image Title: Local Recurrence Within Electronic Surface Brachytherapy Field  Image Description: An elevated, pearly lesion on the left lower eyelid with scleral show on the prosthetic eye of the left lateral lower eyelid, showing conjunctival forniceal shortening with extrusion of the prosthesis and cicatricial shortening of the lower eyelid skin- Pathology:  Initial shave biopsy revealed nodular BCC of the left cheek  Subsequent biopsy specimen showed infiltrative pattern with no signs of fibrosis or inflammatory infiltrate",
